1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,955
A new version passes every test in the test environment.

2
00:00:03,955 --> 00:00:05,930
The team ships it to production.

3
00:00:05,930 --> 00:00:06,793
And it breaks.

4
00:00:07,343 --> 00:00:07,960
Same code,

5
00:00:07,960 --> 00:00:09,193
same version number.

6
00:00:09,193 --> 00:00:10,242
So what happened?

7
00:00:10,242 --> 00:00:12,400
Somebody rebuilt it for production.

8
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:15,791
And the rebuild was not the same thing that was tested.

9
00:00:16,341 --> 00:00:17,963
A second team builds once,

10
00:00:17,963 --> 00:00:21,207
and moves that exact file through every environment.

11
00:00:21,207 --> 00:00:22,891
Nothing changes on the way.

12
00:00:23,441 --> 00:00:24,901
Today: build artifacts,

13
00:00:24,901 --> 00:00:25,727
environments,

14
00:00:25,727 --> 00:00:27,123
and release promotion.

15
00:00:27,123 --> 00:00:30,425
How a build travels from a laptop to your customers,

16
00:00:30,425 --> 00:00:31,505
without changing.

17
00:00:33,055 --> 00:00:34,484
Here is what to expect.

18
00:00:35,034 --> 00:00:38,298
One: what a build artifact is, and the rule: build once.

19
00:00:38,848 --> 00:00:42,795
Two: environments, and why the settings live outside the artifact.

20
00:00:43,345 --> 00:00:44,373
Three: promotion,

21
00:00:44,373 --> 00:00:46,851
the moves from dev to test to production,

22
00:00:46,851 --> 00:00:48,785
and rollback when it goes wrong.

23
00:00:49,335 --> 00:00:50,498
Four: a live demo.

24
00:00:50,498 --> 00:00:51,919
We rebuild by mistake,

25
00:00:51,919 --> 00:00:52,953
then build once,

26
00:00:52,953 --> 00:00:54,439
promote it three times,

27
00:00:54,439 --> 00:00:56,119
and catch a tampered file.

28
00:00:56,669 --> 00:00:59,805
This is lesson four of the DevOps fundamentals domain.

29
00:01:01,855 --> 00:01:03,519
Start with the build artifact.

30
00:01:04,069 --> 00:01:05,820
Your source code is text.

31
00:01:05,820 --> 00:01:06,520
To run it,

32
00:01:06,520 --> 00:01:07,430
you build it.

33
00:01:07,430 --> 00:01:10,231
The result is the artifact: one package,

34
00:01:10,231 --> 00:01:10,651
a jar,

35
00:01:10,651 --> 00:01:11,072
a zip,

36
00:01:11,072 --> 00:01:12,332
a container image,

37
00:01:12,332 --> 00:01:13,242
ready to run.

38
00:01:13,792 --> 00:01:19,126
An artifact has a name and a version, for example shop, one point four point zero.

39
00:01:19,676 --> 00:01:21,385
It also has a fingerprint.

40
00:01:21,385 --> 00:01:22,108
A checksum,

41
00:01:22,108 --> 00:01:24,080
computed from the exact bytes.

42
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:25,131
Change one byte,

43
00:01:25,131 --> 00:01:26,972
and the fingerprint changes.

44
00:01:27,522 --> 00:01:31,980
So the fingerprint answers one question: is this the same file we tested?

45
00:01:33,530 --> 00:01:35,599
Now the golden rule: build once.

46
00:01:36,149 --> 00:01:37,858
The mistake looks innocent.

47
00:01:37,858 --> 00:01:38,744
Build for dev.

48
00:01:38,744 --> 00:01:40,073
Build again for test.

49
00:01:40,073 --> 00:01:41,781
Build again for production.

50
00:01:42,331 --> 00:01:44,444
Each build can differ: a timestamp,

51
00:01:44,444 --> 00:01:45,410
a newer library,

52
00:01:45,410 --> 00:01:46,617
a different machine.

53
00:01:46,617 --> 00:01:47,402
Three builds,

54
00:01:47,402 --> 00:01:48,730
three different files,

55
00:01:48,730 --> 00:01:50,481
with the same version number.

56
00:01:51,031 --> 00:01:55,681
What you tested is not what you ship. And nobody can tell, because the names match.

57
00:01:56,231 --> 00:02:01,543
The fix: build one time, store that artifact, and move the same artifact everywhere.

58
00:02:02,093 --> 00:02:06,488
Same bytes in dev, in test, and in production. The fingerprint proves it.

59
00:02:08,038 --> 00:02:11,473
Where does the artifact live? In an artifact repository.

60
00:02:12,023 --> 00:02:14,180
Think of it as a library for builds.

61
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:15,618
Every version is stored,

62
00:02:15,618 --> 00:02:17,655
with its name and its fingerprint.

63
00:02:18,205 --> 00:02:18,799
For jars,

64
00:02:18,799 --> 00:02:20,514
that is a tool like Nexus.

65
00:02:20,514 --> 00:02:21,900
For container images,

66
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:22,626
a registry,

67
00:02:22,626 --> 00:02:23,682
like Docker Hub.

68
00:02:23,682 --> 00:02:25,991
You will use both in this bootcamp.

69
00:02:26,541 --> 00:02:28,756
One rule: versions are immutable.

70
00:02:28,756 --> 00:02:31,710
Once one point four point zero is published,

71
00:02:31,710 --> 00:02:33,119
nobody overwrites it.

72
00:02:33,119 --> 00:02:34,797
A fix gets a new version.

73
00:02:35,347 --> 00:02:39,294
Do not deploy from someone's laptop. Deploy from the repository.

74
00:02:40,844 --> 00:02:44,087
Next, environments. Places where the artifact runs.

75
00:02:44,637 --> 00:02:46,072
Dev is for developers,

76
00:02:46,072 --> 00:02:47,377
and it breaks often.

77
00:02:47,377 --> 00:02:48,943
Test checks it properly.

78
00:02:48,943 --> 00:02:51,031
Staging is a copy of production,

79
00:02:51,031 --> 00:02:52,466
for a final rehearsal.

80
00:02:52,466 --> 00:02:54,685
Production is where customers are.

81
00:02:55,235 --> 00:02:58,145
Each environment differs: a different database,

82
00:02:58,145 --> 00:02:59,384
a different address,

83
00:02:59,384 --> 00:03:00,375
debug on or off,

84
00:03:00,375 --> 00:03:01,613
different passwords.

85
00:03:02,163 --> 00:03:05,164
So how do you use one artifact in four places?

86
00:03:05,164 --> 00:03:07,056
The settings live outside it.

87
00:03:07,056 --> 00:03:08,883
The artifact stays the same,

88
00:03:08,883 --> 00:03:12,275
and each environment supplies its own configuration.

89
00:03:12,825 --> 00:03:14,212
Never bake a password,

90
00:03:14,212 --> 00:03:15,599
or a database address,

91
00:03:15,599 --> 00:03:16,733
into the artifact.

92
00:03:16,733 --> 00:03:18,372
Put it in the environment.

93
00:03:18,922 --> 00:03:22,655
Same artifact, different configuration. That is the whole trick.

94
00:03:24,205 --> 00:03:26,765
Now promotion. Moving the artifact up.

95
00:03:27,315 --> 00:03:32,862
Promote means: the same artifact, to the next environment, only after it passes the gate.

96
00:03:33,412 --> 00:03:35,136
The artifact goes to dev.

97
00:03:35,136 --> 00:03:36,585
Automated tests pass,

98
00:03:36,585 --> 00:03:38,034
and it moves to test.

99
00:03:38,034 --> 00:03:39,207
More checks pass,

100
00:03:39,207 --> 00:03:40,863
and it moves to staging.

101
00:03:40,863 --> 00:03:41,207
Then,

102
00:03:41,207 --> 00:03:41,966
production.

103
00:03:42,516 --> 00:03:43,597
Before every move,

104
00:03:43,597 --> 00:03:45,218
you verify the fingerprint.

105
00:03:45,218 --> 00:03:46,059
If it differs,

106
00:03:46,059 --> 00:03:46,599
you stop.

107
00:03:46,599 --> 00:03:48,340
Something changed on the way.

108
00:03:48,890 --> 00:03:50,671
Each gate can be automatic,

109
00:03:50,671 --> 00:03:51,397
like tests,

110
00:03:51,397 --> 00:03:52,056
or manual,

111
00:03:52,056 --> 00:03:53,639
like a person approving.

112
00:03:53,639 --> 00:03:56,080
Lesson 146 showed you both.

113
00:03:56,630 --> 00:04:01,195
A promoted artifact is a trusted artifact. The evidence travels with it.

114
00:04:02,745 --> 00:04:04,942
What if production goes wrong? Rollback.

115
00:04:05,492 --> 00:04:07,514
Because every version is stored,

116
00:04:07,514 --> 00:04:08,651
and never changed,

117
00:04:08,651 --> 00:04:09,852
going back is easy.

118
00:04:09,852 --> 00:04:12,063
Deploy the previous artifact again.

119
00:04:12,613 --> 00:04:13,251
No rebuild,

120
00:04:13,251 --> 00:04:13,946
no guessing.

121
00:04:13,946 --> 00:04:16,091
You already know that version worked,

122
00:04:16,091 --> 00:04:18,352
because it ran in production yesterday.

123
00:04:18,902 --> 00:04:21,007
That is why you keep old versions.

124
00:04:21,007 --> 00:04:24,598
And why fast rollback is the safety net for fast releases.

125
00:04:25,148 --> 00:04:29,990
Think of rollback as a seat belt. You hope not to use it, and you are glad it is there.

126
00:04:31,540 --> 00:04:35,657
Go to the demo folder.

127
00:04:37,207 --> 00:04:39,106
Look around.

128
00:04:39,656 --> 00:04:43,240
A source folder, three configuration files, and three scripts.

129
00:04:43,790 --> 00:04:49,337
The mistake first.

130
00:04:49,887 --> 00:04:54,665
Three builds, three different fingerprints. Same version number, three different files.

131
00:04:55,215 --> 00:04:59,589
Build once.

132
00:05:00,139 --> 00:05:03,680
One build, one fingerprint. Remember the first few characters.

133
00:05:04,230 --> 00:05:08,390
Promote to dev.

134
00:05:08,940 --> 00:05:11,927
Same fingerprint, and the dev settings: debug on.

135
00:05:12,477 --> 00:05:13,714
Promote to test.

136
00:05:15,564 --> 00:05:16,652
Promote to prod.

137
00:05:18,702 --> 00:05:20,784
The same fingerprint three times.

138
00:05:20,784 --> 00:05:21,919
The same artifact.

139
00:05:21,919 --> 00:05:24,947
Only the database and the debug setting changed,

140
00:05:24,947 --> 00:05:27,470
because they come from each environment.

141
00:05:28,020 --> 00:05:32,777
Tamper with it.

142
00:05:33,327 --> 00:05:35,226
Try to promote.

143
00:05:36,376 --> 00:05:36,682
Stop.

144
00:05:36,682 --> 00:05:38,766
The fingerprint no longer matches,

145
00:05:38,766 --> 00:05:40,175
so the move is refused.

146
00:05:40,175 --> 00:05:42,136
This check just saved a release.

147
00:05:42,686 --> 00:05:46,569
Build clean.

148
00:05:47,119 --> 00:05:48,228
Promote again.

149
00:05:50,178 --> 00:05:53,442
A new artifact, a new fingerprint, verified, and live.

150
00:05:53,992 --> 00:05:57,832
Write it down.

151
00:06:08,582 --> 00:06:11,185
Check the file.

152
00:06:12,735 --> 00:06:15,380
Your practice task: build once, and promote it.

153
00:06:15,930 --> 00:06:19,386
One: run the naive script, and see three different fingerprints.

154
00:06:19,936 --> 00:06:20,999
Two: build once,

155
00:06:20,999 --> 00:06:22,328
then promote to dev,

156
00:06:22,328 --> 00:06:22,660
test,

157
00:06:22,660 --> 00:06:23,258
and prod.

158
00:06:23,258 --> 00:06:25,782
The fingerprint must match every time.

159
00:06:26,332 --> 00:06:30,172
Three: tamper with the artifact, and try to promote. It must stop.

160
00:06:30,722 --> 00:06:34,071
Four: build clean, promote again, and write your three lines.

161
00:06:36,121 --> 00:06:39,342
Where to find the demo files. Pause here, and write it down.

162
00:06:39,892 --> 00:06:45,567
One: the repository, github.com/it-waves/devops-ai.

163
00:06:46,117 --> 00:06:50,256
Two: the folder academy/lesson148-demo.

164
00:06:50,806 --> 00:06:52,105
Three: three scripts,

165
00:06:52,105 --> 00:06:52,477
naive,

166
00:06:52,477 --> 00:06:52,848
build,

167
00:06:52,848 --> 00:06:53,591
and promote,

168
00:06:53,591 --> 00:06:54,581
a source folder,

169
00:06:54,581 --> 00:06:55,757
three config files,

170
00:06:55,757 --> 00:06:57,675
and a read me with the answers.

171
00:06:58,225 --> 00:06:59,930
Four: clone the repository,

172
00:06:59,930 --> 00:07:01,192
and copy the folder.

173
00:07:01,192 --> 00:07:02,266
You need a shell,

174
00:07:02,266 --> 00:07:02,960
and python.

175
00:07:02,960 --> 00:07:04,476
Nothing else to install.

176
00:07:05,026 --> 00:07:05,761
Five: again,

177
00:07:05,761 --> 00:07:07,110
no lab in this domain.

178
00:07:07,110 --> 00:07:08,459
The practice is plain,

179
00:07:08,459 --> 00:07:10,850
and the answers are on the next screen.

180
00:07:12,900 --> 00:07:15,375
Check yourself. Six points. Tick each one.

181
00:07:15,925 --> 00:07:18,933
One: the naive script shows three different fingerprints.

182
00:07:19,483 --> 00:07:21,680
Two: one build gives one fingerprint.

183
00:07:22,230 --> 00:07:25,323
Three: dev, test, and prod show the same fingerprint.

184
00:07:25,873 --> 00:07:29,137
Four: but each shows its own database and debug setting.

185
00:07:29,687 --> 00:07:32,183
Five: a tampered artifact is refused.

186
00:07:32,733 --> 00:07:35,613
Six: your file has three lines, in your own words.

187
00:07:36,163 --> 00:07:38,638
All six, and you understand the three ideas.

188
00:07:40,688 --> 00:07:42,459
Your turn, on your own machine.

189
00:07:43,009 --> 00:07:44,843
One: copy the demo folder.

190
00:07:45,393 --> 00:07:48,529
Two: run the naive script, and compare the fingerprints.

191
00:07:49,079 --> 00:07:53,218
Three: build once, promote three times, then tamper and watch it stop.

192
00:07:53,768 --> 00:07:55,347
Four: write your three lines.

193
00:07:55,897 --> 00:07:57,458
Five: tick the six points.

194
00:07:57,458 --> 00:08:01,422
